RecoverKit third-party notices
==============================

Office XOR regression fixtures
  tests/RecoverKit.Tests/Fixtures/OfficeXor/poi-xor-abc.xls is from Apache POI test-data;
  excel95-kit95.xls, excel95-long-user.xls, word6-kit6.doc and word95-word95.doc.gz
  are modified POI samples.
  https://github.com/apache/poi/tree/trunk/test-data - Apache License 2.0.
  The fixture folder contains the upstream license, notice and source details.

RecoverKit's Windows installer, portable zip and macOS app contain RecoverKit, Bouncy Castle
(license below), the Avalonia UI framework and the libraries it ships with (listed below) and,
in the self-contained builds, the .NET runtime (MIT License, https://github.com/dotnet/runtime).
The macOS app also contains hashcat. The other components below are not bundled: RecoverKit
downloads them from their official sources on first use, checks their SHA-256, and stores them
in the user's profile. Each remains under its own license.

Avalonia 12.1.6 (bundled)
  https://avaloniaui.net - MIT License. Includes SkiaSharp (MIT, Skia: BSD 3-Clause),
  HarfBuzzSharp (MIT, HarfBuzz: Old MIT) and, on Windows, ANGLE (BSD 3-Clause).

hashcat 7.1.2
  https://hashcat.net - MIT License.
  Downloaded on Windows and Linux. The macOS app bundles it instead (hashcat.net publishes no
  macOS binary): RecoverKit.app/Contents/Resources/hashcat is built unmodified from the official
  hashcat 7.1.2 source release by macos/build-hashcat.sh (only the x86_64 slice is dropped from
  the build). Its license and the licenses of the libraries it includes (zlib, LZMA SDK, xxHash,
  OpenCL headers, unrar, sse2neon, and others) are in that folder's docs/ and docs/license_libs/.

7-Zip reduced console extractor (7zr.exe), Windows only, when no 7-Zip is installed
  https://www.7-zip.org - GNU LGPL.

LZMA metadata decoder (built in)
  src/RecoverKit.Core/Formats/SevenZipLzma.cs adapts the LZMA1 decoder from Igor Pavlov's
  LZMA SDK 23.01 (https://www.7-zip.org/sdk.html), placed in the public domain.

Access test fixtures (test data only)
  tests/RecoverKit.Tests/Fixtures/Access/*.bin contain header-page excerpts from Jackcess
  and Jackcess Encrypt, Apache License 2.0, https://github.com/jahlborn/jackcess and
  https://github.com/jahlborn/jackcessencrypt. Sources and modifications are listed in that
  folder's README.md; the upstream license and notice are included there.

Intel CPU Runtime for OpenCL Applications (intel-opencl-rt) and oneAPI Threading
Building Blocks (tbb), Windows only, optional, after the user accepts Intel's license
  https://pypi.org/project/intel-opencl-rt/ - Intel End User License Agreement.
  https://pypi.org/project/tbb/ - Apache License 2.0.

Stay's ZipCrypto attack (built in, not bundled)
  Michael Stay, "ZIP Attacks with Reduced Known Plaintext", Fast Software Encryption 2001,
  https://www.usenix.org/legacy/events/fse01/stay.html - the divide-and-conquer attack on
  weak rand()-seeded ZipCrypto encryption headers (WinZip <= 8 / Info-ZIP-derived zippers).
  The Full Disclosure post "Cracking WinZip archives by exploiting weak random number
  generation" (2003, http://seclists.org/fulldisclosure/2003/Jul/152) and the reference
  implementation in Info-ZIP's crypt.c (http://www.infozip.org, the BSD-style Info-ZIP license)
  were used to reproduce the header generation faithfully.

Built-in word lists
  src/RecoverKit.Core/Attacks/Resources/SmallEnglish.txt and MediumEnglish.txt are a selection
  from the macOS /usr/share/dict/words list (Webster's Second International Dictionary, 1934,
  public domain), preceded by a short list of common passwords.

Office test fixtures (tests only)
  tests/RecoverKit.Tests/Fixtures/Office/rc4-*.xls, cryptoapi*.xls and cryptoapi-empty.doc
  are modified Apache POI test-data files (Apache Software Foundation, Apache License 2.0):
  https://github.com/apache/poi/blob/trunk/test-data/spreadsheet/password.xls
  https://github.com/apache/poi/blob/trunk/test-data/spreadsheet/35897-type4.xls
  https://github.com/apache/poi/blob/trunk/test-data/document/password_password_cryptoapi.doc
  Resaved with Apache POI 5.4.1 using the default/empty password and, for cryptoapi40, a
  40-bit key. The Apache license is in tests/RecoverKit.Tests/Fixtures/Office/LICENSE.txt.
  Other Office fixtures were generated for RecoverKit with openpyxl, msoffcrypto-tool,
  Apache POI and LibreOffice; no third-party implementation code is included.

